  'body'        => <<<'HTML'
<p>The right multi-purpose furniture for studio apartments lets one room handle sleeping, working, eating and hosting without feeling cramped. Focus on pieces that convert (sofa beds, folding desks), hide storage (ottomans, bed risers) or shrink when not in use (drop-leaf tables, nesting tables). Here is how to choose them and lay out your studio so it actually works day to day.</p>

<h2>Why studios need furniture that does double duty</h2>
<p>A studio has no walls to separate activities, so every piece of furniture is visible all the time. Bulky single-purpose items eat floor space fast and leave the room feeling cluttered. Multi-purpose pieces give you flexibility: the same square footage becomes an office at 10 a.m., a dining room at 7 p.m. and a bedroom at night.</p>
<p>Before buying anything, measure the room, note where outlets, windows and the entry door are, and sketch a rough floor plan on paper. It sounds like overkill, but it prevents the most common mistake: buying a sofa bed that blocks the closet when it is open.</p>

<h2>1. Futons and sofa beds</h2>
<p>A sofa that becomes a bed is the classic studio move for good reason. Modern futons and sleeper sofas look far better than the dorm versions most of us remember, and many fold flat in seconds.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Measure open and closed.</strong> Leave a clear walking path even when it is folded out.</li>
<li><strong>Check the mattress.</strong> If you will sleep on it every night, a thicker mattress matters more than the frame style.</li>
<li><strong>Look for storage.</strong> Some models include a drawer or under-seat compartment for bedding.</li>
</ul>
<p>If you sleep on it nightly, a well-reviewed [[amz:futon|futon]] with a thick mattress is a solid pick that will also serve as guest seating later.</p>

<h2>2. Folding and wall-friendly desks</h2>
<p>If you work or study from home, a dedicated desk helps you mentally switch on and off. A folding desk can be tucked flat against the wall or slid behind the sofa when you are done, which reclaims floor space for the evening.</p>
<p>Wall-mounted fold-down desks are great, but they need screws in the wall, so ask your landlord first. For a no-drill option, a freestanding [[amz:folding_desk|folding desk]] or a leaning ladder desk with built-in shelves gives you a workspace without touching the drywall.</p>
<!--products:futon,folding_desk,storage_ottoman|Space-saving pieces for a studio-->

<h2>3. Storage ottomans and benches</h2>
<p>An ottoman with a lift-off lid is seating, a footrest, a coffee table (add a tray) and a blanket chest all in one. Place one at the foot of the bed for linens, another by the door for shoes and bags. Benches with cubbies underneath do the same job along a wall or at a dining table.</p>
<div class="callout"><p><strong>Pro tip:</strong> Pick ottomans that are the same height as your sofa seat. They double as extra seating for guests without looking mismatched.</p></div>

<h2>4. Murphy beds, daybeds and lofted beds</h2>
<p>A Murphy bed folds up into a cabinet and frees the whole sleeping zone for daytime use. Some even have a desk or shelves on the front. The catch for renters is that most need to be anchored to the wall, so get written approval and plan on repairing anchor holes when you leave.</p>
<p>A daybed is a simpler alternative: it reads as a sofa with pillows along the back and sleeps like a twin bed. If your ceilings are tall, a loft bed lets you put a desk or reading nook underneath.</p>

<h2>5. Drop-leaf, extendable and nesting tables</h2>
<p>A drop-leaf table can sit against a wall as a slim console, then open to seat two or four. Extendable tables do the same with a center leaf. Nesting side tables tuck together most of the time and spread out when friends come over. A small bar-height table can also work as a standing desk.</p>

<h2>6. Use the space under and above everything</h2>
<p>In a studio, the floor is precious, so go under and up:</p>
<ul>
<li>Rolling [[amz:under_bed_storage|under-bed storage bins]] hold off-season clothes, extra bedding and shoes.</li>
<li>Bed risers add several inches of clearance for bigger bins.</li>
<li>Tall, narrow bookshelves use vertical space without a wide footprint.</li>
<li>Over-the-door organizers turn the back of the bathroom or closet door into storage.</li>
</ul>
<!--products:under_bed_storage,area_rug,command_strips|Storage and zoning helpers-->

<h2>Laying out your studio in zones</h2>
<p>Think of your studio as three or four zones: sleep, living, work and eat. A few simple tactics make the boundaries clear:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Rugs define areas.</strong> Put an area rug under the living zone and another under the dining table.</li>
<li><strong>Use soft dividers.</strong> An open bookshelf, a sofa with its back to the bed, or a curtain on a ceiling tension rod separates spaces without blocking light.</li>
<li><strong>Light each zone.</strong> A floor lamp by the sofa, a desk lamp in the office corner and a bedside lamp make each area feel like its own room.</li>
<li><strong>Keep the path clear.</strong> Leave a straight walking route from the door to the windows and bathroom.</li>
</ol>

<table>
<thead><tr><th>Piece</th><th>Jobs it does</th><th>Renter-friendly?</th></tr></thead>
