<tr><td>Folding desk</td><td>Workspace, console, vanity</td><td>Yes (freestanding)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Storage ottoman</td><td>Seating, table, storage</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
<tr><td>Murphy bed</td><td>Bed, cabinet, sometimes desk</td><td>Ask first (wall anchors)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Drop-leaf table</td><td>Console, dining table</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<h2>Small-space lessons for your future home</h2>
<p>Plenty of our readers live in a studio while saving up to build. That is not wasted time. Living small shows you exactly how much space you really use, which can save real money when you design a house. People who have lived in a studio often realize they would rather have a smaller, well-planned home with great storage than extra rooms they never enter. Ideas like those in <a href="/the-art-of-home-design-where-passion-meets-comfort/">the art of home design</a> and the "build better, not bigger" approach become much easier to apply. When you get there, our guide on <a href="/how-to-choose-a-floor-plan-for-your-new-home/">how to choose a floor plan</a> walks through it step by step.</p>

<h2>Next steps</h2>
<p>Start with the one piece that solves your biggest pain point, usually the bed or the desk, and build out from there. If you are renting while you plan a build, read <a href="/how-to-turn-your-first-apartment-into-a-stepping-stone-for-home-investments/">how to turn your first apartment into a stepping stone</a> and check out our <a href="/free-training/">free training</a> on building a new home.</p>
